WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.720
I had a chief of staff who I was lucky to&nbsp;
work with for a year named Kimberly Cotton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:09.600
and she was the chief of staff to the&nbsp;
CEO of this Fortune 500 company. I met&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:14.800
her backstage speaking at a large event for&nbsp;
that company. Turns out her CEO was retiring.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.560
Anyway, to make a long story short,&nbsp;
she was a master question asker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:24.560
and she illuminated for me something that I&nbsp;
had experienced over my life, which is there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:31.280
are three types of questions that you can ask&nbsp;
someone that really actually make a difference,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:35.440
that help shape or edit the future in some&nbsp;
way. And so, in this video, I’m going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:41.840
give you what I believe are three way better&nbsp;
conversation starters that maybe could replace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:48.240
all icebreakers. And I’m going to get really&nbsp;
close and intense to share these three framings.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:53.840
At one level, you can ask possibility questions,&nbsp;
right? What is possible? Sharing a vision for what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.640
is possible in the future. On the next&nbsp;
level, you ask process questions. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:04.640
a little bit more digging into the how—how&nbsp;
do you actually wind up there? And then Kim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:11.280
was amazing at asking practical questions. So I&nbsp;
would share this vision or an idea for something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:17.680
and she was a master of saying, “That’s amazing.&nbsp;
By when would you like to have that done?”

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:22.720
So let me give you three questions that I&nbsp;
really love that you can walk someone through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:27.840
right from possibility into process&nbsp;
into a very practical place. You ready?

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.240
At the possibility level of questions,&nbsp;
you’re really talking about vision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:37.600
right? Getting someone’s larger aim. It doesn’t&nbsp;
need to be hyper-specific. You’re just like, “What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:44.800
do you want?” Right? Those kinds of questions. If&nbsp;
I ask you a question like, “If you could solve one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:51.840
problem in the world, what would it be?” And&nbsp;
so when I hear somebody’s response to that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:56.960
what I’m now getting curious about—if I’m&nbsp;
kind of hovering—I almost picture these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:02.080
possibility, process, and practical levels&nbsp;
like a spiral, where you’ve got to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:06.000
in the land of possibility, in the land of&nbsp;
why, what’s the point, what’s the big vision.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.480
Otherwise, what happens too often is&nbsp;
when we try to get action-oriented,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:15.440
we ask practical questions. Practical questions&nbsp;
are great at generating a bunch of activity that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:19.520
leads to nowhere because they’re not tied to&nbsp;
some larger vision. And so, when you start with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:25.200
the level of possibility and you spiral down&nbsp;
to how it’s going to get done—process—when I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:30.960
ask you a question like, “What brings you joy?”&nbsp;
and then I’m able to combine that with, “Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:36.000
you want to solve this problem, and what brings&nbsp;
you joy?” I can now start to think, how do those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:41.200
intersect? How might you combine those? So there’s&nbsp;
some reflection and debriefing happening there.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.680
And then, really practically, what is it that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.920
want to start doing? So you can see&nbsp;
this is just three question examples.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:53.680
I got so on about this idea that questions&nbsp;
are the edit button to the future, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:03:02.080
questions that are future-focused spark&nbsp;
the tiny actions, which is us verbalizing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:07.920
us creating new neural pathways that lead to&nbsp;
actual implementation out in the world. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:14.400
what I did was spend a whole heap of time packing&nbsp;
60 possibility, process, and practical questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:22.880
into what I believe is the coolest tool to replace&nbsp;
all other icebreakers or conversation starters.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:26.240
It is the Future-Focused We&nbsp;
Connect Cards, and it’s really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:33.680
simply a deck designed to help people talk their&nbsp;
way into a more compelling, interesting tomorrow.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:39.680
The no-prep takeaway here is that when you’re&nbsp;
in a group, if you can start with possibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:44.960
questions as a conversation starter, and then take&nbsp;
their answers to that and add a process-oriented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:52.080
question like, “How do you like going about that?”&nbsp;
and then drop it into really practical terms like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:56.880
“What’s the tiniest action you might want to&nbsp;
take in the next week to make that happen?”—that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.040
sequence of possibility to process to&nbsp;
practical is transformative for a group.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:05.360
And so, if you want to start conversations that&nbsp;
actually go somewhere, that make a difference,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:10.560
that is your formula. If you want to pick up a&nbsp;
deck of these cards, they’re available on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:14.720
internet. I guess they’re sponsoring this&nbsp;
video. I don’t know. Have an awesome day.

